Z||000000||Voyd||11-07-1999||07:48 AM||||my car didnt come with any stereo in it, the previous owner just ripped it out before he sold it.  i wanted a cdplayer instead of a normal tape deck anyways.<P>so to all you guys who replaced the tape deck with a nice cdplayer, any brands/models in particular you find kick ass in the fiero (stuff like matching red glow)?<P>thanks||||reg||6
Z||000001||Formula||11-07-1999||08:03 AM||||the JVC Kamileon is VERY cool. Plus you can change the color to red in about 5 secs. Goto a stereo store and check it out. When you turn it on and/or off the buttons flip up and hide so a robber thinks that its not there..........2nd choice would be a high end sony, they change color to. They have a lot of settings on them......I personally have a full space Jensen. You have to take the car out of park to change a CD. It is Green only, which doesn't look to bad. Have Fun looking for a stereo.........Also, if you ar looking for a subwoofer look at previous stereo posts for info. Good luck||||reg||1
Z||000002||Voyd||11-09-1999||10:08 AM||||do you need one of those special fit GM/Chrysler model cd players, or will any cd player fir in there?||||reg||1
Z||000003||TOM||11-09-1999||12:06 PM||||I have a regular clarion in mine. I made a plug so I didnt have to cut into the factory wiring. I know you can buy them but it was easy fo me to make.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Scott2M4||11-09-1999||12:43 PM||||When shopping for an aftermarket CD player for my '87, I used Crutchfield's website (www.crutchfield.com) - VERY COOL!!!  You can customize the display to only show equipment that will fit your vehicle and they have a feature that allows you to compare the different equipment.<P>On top of that, they provide free wiring harnesses, installation instructions/tips and, in some cases, dash kits to make the GM/Chrysler "double-din" openings fit the "din-sized" head unit.<P>I highly recommend them!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Voyd||11-09-1999||04:28 PM||||thanks Scott, great site.<P>so, do Fieros have a double DIN sized hole for the unit? if i get a regular sized unit, does it come with the necessary bracket to fit it in?<P>if i go with a special sized GM/chrysler model, is installation pretty much straight forward?<P>ive never changed a radio before at all. i wouldnt know what im doing.<P>thanks||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||11-09-1999||05:20 PM||||The Fiero does not use the double DIN size stereo. It uses a unique size that is in between DIN and double DIN. A DIN (standard size CD player) will work, but you must use an inexpensive installation adapter kit. A GM size CD player like the ones Pioneer or Jensen makes fits into the dash without any extra stuff to buy or install (except maybe for the wiring harness adapter plug).||||reg||1
Z||000007||88 Fiero||11-09-1999||05:23 PM||||If you want to stay with the stock look...try looking for a Trans Am or a Sunfire Delco CD/Cassette player... They do sound awesome specially if you have the subwoofer unit. I have the Trans Am Delco head and it's great...got a pair of 5 1/2 Bazooka Bass Tubes behind the driver and passenger seat and it sounds better than the 6 inch of the factory sub-woofer.||||reg||1
Z||000008||ggarsk||11-09-1999||05:59 PM||||Crutchfields is nice if you want to check out different units and their stats. But I got my cd player locally for $30 less then they wanted for the same one and I didn't have to pay for shipping or have to wait for it. Best thing to do is go to a good,reputable stereo store and ask qyestions. you don't have to buy from them,but it will give you an idea of what you want. Installation isn't that hard.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Racer 1||11-10-1999||04:49 PM||||Crutchfield is a awesome because you get free install kit and free wiring kit plus detailed instructions how to the radio.<BR>GO HERE <A HREF="http://www.crutchfield.com/S-0XudRM9N1h7/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.crutchfield.com/S-0XudRM9N1h7/index.html</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://fierorob.homepage.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://fierorob.homepage.com/</A> <P>||||reg||14
